---
title: Maximus the Confessor Crash Course
description: A self-contained study guide to Maximus the Confessor: the patristic synthesis of both Logos axes, the logoi doctrine, the natural/gnomic will, the mutilation of 662, and the conciliar reversal of 681.
date: 2026
tags: [study guide, theology]
---

If you have followed the arc this far &mdash; through [Origen](/posts/origen-crash-course.html), condemned three hundred years after his death and recovered through fog; through the [Cappadocians](/posts/cappadocians-crash-course.html), who quietly carried theosis forward without anyone catching on; through [Pseudo-Dionysius](/posts/pseudo-dionysius-crash-course.html), whose apophatic theology survived because the church mistook him for one of Paul's converts &mdash; you are probably sitting with the same question I sat with for a long time. If the participation tradition really was the intellectual core of early Christianity, where did it go after 553? Did it only survive underground &mdash; borrowed names, unauthorized Latin translations, condemned syntheses, the line running through [Eriugena](/posts/eriugena-crash-course.html) to Eckhart? Or is there a stronger claim available somewhere &mdash; that it kept maturing right through the orthodox center, in the most canonical Greek-East line, never suppressed, never patched in later, formally taught?

The answer to that question is one person. And the strangest fact in patristic history is that the man the Byzantine state crippled in 662 wrote, posthumously and without revision, the conciliar definition that overruled the emperor who broke him.

Maximus the Confessor (c. 580&ndash;662) died in exile in Lazica &mdash; modern Tsageri, in the mountains of western Georgia &mdash; on 13 August 662. The empire had cut out his tongue and severed his right hand a few months earlier in Constantinople, in a public mutilation designed to silence the two organs of his theological work. He died of the injuries, in a province at the far edge of what was left of Roman power, after refusing to sign the emperor's preferred Christology. Nineteen years later, in 681, the Sixth Ecumenical Council &mdash; Constantinople III &mdash; adopted his exact technical distinctions, essentially verbatim, as the orthodox definition of how Christ wills. The emperor who had mutilated him was dead. The pope who had compromised with the imperial position was anathematized by name in the council's own acts. Maximus is the only patristic figure whose precise technical vocabulary became conciliar doctrine in his own terms within a generation of his death.

That asymmetry &mdash; mutilated by the state, canonized by the church, his terminology written into orthodox law &mdash; is the spine of everything else. It is the structural difference between Origen and Maximus. Origen got the participation tradition condemned. Maximus got the participation tradition canonized. The participation and apophatic axes were not only a condemned underground. They were also the formal teaching at the orthodox center, articulated by a man whose body the state could break but whose textual record it could not.

